  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 AM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nAn amplifying ridge across the Great Plains into southern Canada \nkeeps the summer-like temperatures in place this afternoon. \nTemperatures climb into the mid-80s across the region under mostly \nsunny skies. These temperatures are approximately 15 degrees above \nnormal for this time of year and just shy of record territory. Wind \nspeeds will gradually increase later this afternoon as the surface \npressure gradient begins to tighten as a trough digs across the \nPacific Northwest. With the increasing south winds, temperatures \nremain milder tonight, hovering in the 60s across the area through \nSaturday morning.\n\nSaturday will be the warmest and windiest day of the forecast \nperiod. The low-level thermal ridge and pressure gradient continues \nto strengthen and tighten as a large trough digs over the West Coast \nand as Hurricane Isaias makes landfall in the Southeast. As a \nresult, south winds increase in speed with peak afternoon gusts of \n30-40+ mph likely. High temperatures climb into the upper 80s to \nnear 90 across the area. Several records may be challenged across \nthe region with these expected temperatures. Norfolk's forecast high \ntemperature is 89 with a previous record of 87 set in 2024. Omaha's \nrecord of 88 set in 1963 may be challenged with a forecast high of \n89. Lincoln may fall shy of their record high of 91 set in 2024 with \na forecast high of 87. In either event, these temperatures are \nreminiscent of mid-Summer rather than Fall.\n\nIn addition to the Summer-like heat and windy conditions, dry \nweather develops. Afternoon minimum relative humidity plummets to \nbetween 20-40 percent across the area. The strongest winds and \nlowest relative humidity is forecast over northeast Nebraska. An \nelevated fire weather threat may develop, especially since this \nregion missed out on the majority of September's rainfall. \n\nTemperatures and wind speeds begin to decrease on Sunday as the \npressure gradient begins to slacken and low-level thermal ridge \nshifts east. Summer-like temperatures continue despite this with \nhighs in the low- to mid-80s in the forecast once again. Thankfully, \na cold front arrives early next week, beginning a cooling trend for \nthe region. High temperatures in the 70s begin to return to the \nforecast on Monday and for all by Tuesday. \n\nOur next weather system is set to impact our weather by the middle \nof the week. A trough is expected to eject into the Great Plains and \ninteract with remnant moisture from Hurricane Simon. Ensemble \nguidance is in relatively good agreement that beneficial rainfall \ncan be expected with this system. Widespread rainfall totals between \n0.5\"-1\" appear likely at this juncture. This weather system is \nexpected to remain progressive with another cold front pushing \nthrough the region by week's end. Surface high pressure and Fall-\nlike weather should return to the forecast by next weekend.",
